      "How 'Nano-indenter with in-situ SPM imaging’ is different from ‘Nano indenter with separate AFM imaging? “Nanoindenter with SPM imaging” is an integrated solution, developed and being mastered for over twenty years now. Each of our Nanoindenter head has the capability of surface scanning due to the engineering solution where the nanoindentation head is mounted on the piezo-scanner. • With respect to Hysitron “in-situ SPM” imaging, the same transducer and the diamond tip which is used for nanoindentation is used for high resolution SPM imaging. This allows the user to select an area to scan and image with the same reference frame as the nanoindentation. This allows positioning of the indent to be within 10nm of the target selected. This is impossible by having two different instruments or heads with two different reference frames and one doing the SPM imaging and the other one doing the",
      "impossible by having two different instruments or heads with two different reference frames and one doing the SPM imaging and the other one doing the nanoindentation. The mechanical offsets between the heads and the poor positioning capability of the large mechanical frames will not allow this. • With Hysitron In-situ SPM imaging and nanoindentation, the time to pre scan the surface, to do nanoindentation and do the post scan is very less and is fully automated which is very time consuming and difficult to do with two separate instrument heads. • With in-situ SPM imaging it is possible to scan an area of 1 micron size and place an array of indent or custom selected multiple location indent within that 1 micron size area . This is impossible with imaging with a separate AFM and indenting with a separate indentation head. • With In-situ SPM imaging capability, the sample preparation is",
      "ble with imaging with a separate AFM and indenting with a separate indentation head. • With In-situ SPM imaging capability, the sample preparation is not a major issue as it is possible to image even unpolished samples and select an area which is nice and smooth and do a mechanical test. It is also possible to do this on a curved surface. This is very useful for biological samples like bones, dental materials, tissues, wood materials, concrete materials, geological materials etc which are difficult to polish are prepare. In-situ SPM imaging has been in use on studying these materials by many researchers for many years. Example paper on cement is attached. • With 2D transducer and In-situ SPM imaging it is possible to do quantitative scanning nano wear experiments within 1 micron size location and we can do that within 10nm positioning accuracy. This allows for very local wear studies",
      "ning nano wear experiments within 1 micron size location and we can do that within 10nm positioning accuracy. This allows for very local wear studies and even individual phases within a microstructure. This is impossible with having separate AFM and nanoindentation head. • With Hysitron in-situ SPM imaging and nanoindentation, the noise floor of 30nN or less is guaranteed on the customer site. This allows for very sensitive true nanoscale indentation or deformation studies which can be imaged. For example with Husitron’s technique it is possible to do indent on a monolayer thin film with 10nm positioning capability and study the deformation mechanism, atomic step edges, phase transformation and other defects. It is possible to do in-situ SPM imaging on a nanostructure and precisely do a mechanical test on the structure. This is not possible with a separate AFM and Nanoindenter",
      "in-situ SPM imaging on a nanostructure and precisely do a mechanical test on the structure. This is not possible with a separate AFM and Nanoindenter combination • In-situ SPM imaging capability can be extended to quantitative nanoscale resolution mechanical property mapping. This allows quantitative elastic modulus map of an area at nanoscale. For example it is possible to map the modulus of 1 micron area quantitatively with in-situ SPM imaging in dynamic mode. This is impossible with any other technique or with AFM and separate nanoindentation head. Please note that AFM is a qualitative imaging tool and it cannot be used for quantitative mechanical property measurement. Tapping mode imaging is a qualitative non-linear dynamic mode and the images obtained will give some contrast which is not quantitative and very difficult to interpret. For researchers the advantage of in-situ imaging",
      "e images obtained will give some contrast which is not quantitative and very difficult to interpret. For researchers the advantage of in-situ imaging are The advantages, among others are: a. ability of planning research more reasonably – seeing what is being investigated mechanically allows to limit the number of errors in reasoning or erroneous hypothesis b. ability to understand and interpret mechanical results more easily (we see where the indent was placed so we can draw the conclusions immediately about material behavior or discard the result due to some influences of the dirt, surface irregularities etc) c. ability to able to examine the deformation of the material such as pile – up or sink – in and finally fracture of the material with high precision and avoiding errors related to handling the sample between the instruments. d. Ability to perform the research faster and in fact",
      "with high precision and avoiding errors related to handling the sample between the instruments. d. Ability to perform the research faster and in fact cheaper– to understand material behavior and perform statistical analysis smaller number of tests needs to be performed (with in-situ SPM option) as being able to observe the surface before and after test, there will be less mechanical data that needs to be discarded. e. NanoWear: the integrated solution of the in-situ SPM imaging can be also utilized for the nanotribological studies. The scanning force is set to be high enough deform (wear off) the sample surface (please see the presentation f. The in-situ SPM imaging technique is a base for many other testing techniques that are upgradable options, for example Modulus Mapping technique that allows obtaining maps (images) of the viscoelastic properties of the materials"
