      "if the user is right-handed (Figure 3). 4. The probe has a square mount just below the point, which fits into the probe mounting tool (Figure 4). 5. Place the square portion of the probe into the probe tool, while holding the tool upright at all time (Figure 5). The probe is only held in the tool by gravity and friction, so be careful that the probe does not fall out of the tool. SERVICE DOCUMENT M I N N E A P O L I S , M N 5 5 3 4 4 T E L : 1 9 52 8 3 5 6 3 6 6 FA X : 1 9 52 8 3 5 6 1 6 6 W W W. H Y S I T R O N . C O M -1 of 2 -6. Place the threaded end of the probe onto the threads of the transducer at an angle and then tilt the tool horizontally to prevent the probe from falling out of the tool (Figure 6). 7. Gently turn the probe counter-clockwise until a slight click is felt when the lag of the threads fall into place. This is to avoid cross-threading of the threads of the screw.",
      "ter-clockwise until a slight click is felt when the lag of the threads fall into place. This is to avoid cross-threading of the threads of the screw. 8. Gently turn the probe clockwise until the torque limiting probe tool will not allow further tightening. 9. Follow steps 1 through 8 in reverse order to remove the probe from the transducer. Never allow the nanoindentation probe tool to hang freely off of the transducer (Figure 7). The user should always be supporting the mass of the tool or damage to the finely calibrated springs within the transducer may result. CSV-T-014 v1.0 Figure 4: Nanoindentation probe mounting tool. Figure 5: Nanoindentation probe resting in probe mounting tool. Figure 6: Installing the nanoindentation probe onto the transducer. Figure 7: Always support the mass of the probe tool. M I N N E A P O L I S , M N 5 5 3 4 4 T E L : 1 9 52 8 3 5 6 3 6 6 FA X : 1 9 52 8",
